{"kind":"request","text":"Security finding to verify and close: a declared application may be named `change`, and the destination's `reserve` handler has no check that the sender belongs to its room. Found by reading the source at main 6afbb5b5 while preparing the publication-from-a-fold note (Draft 2, section 9.1). Read only; nothing was run, and how far such a sender gets was not traced.\n\nWhat the source says:\n1. A handler's `from.under` is compared with the NAME the sender's pinned definition states, not a digest or a platform identity: packages/derive/src/handlers.ts line 54, packages/derive/src/delivery.ts line 240, and `nameUnder` in packages/scope/src/namespace.ts (a declared definition answers `declared.name`).\n2. The validator refuses only a declared name that begins with `platform:` (packages/derive/src/validate/index.ts line 166). The rules scope's `activate` checks only that the name equals the act's `name` field, and the application factory (packages/platform/src/application.ts) refuses only capabilities. So a declared definition named `change` (or `issue`) is admitted and can be established as an application scope of kind `lane`.\n3. The destination's `reserve` handler (packages/platform/src/destination.ts from line 366) is `from: { kind: \"lane\", under: \"change\" }`. Its guards are: the `operation` fact is in the sender's own scope (OWNER, line 180), no publication exists for that operation, and fewer than 65 receipts are owed. No guard compares the sender with the destination's directory or room, though `branch.directory` is held and the `import` and `compromised` handlers use it.\n\nThe concern: a person who can activate a definition and establish an application in ANY room on the service might create a scope under a declared definition named `change`, with acts that record entries of the kinds the destination reads (`merge`, `propose-file`, `propose-manifest`) and a `tell` of `reserve` addressed to another room's destination, whose reference is not secret. Whether the destination's later judgment of the reservation (judgeReservation and the reads around line 1462) stops this, for example by reading approvals under the destination's own room's rules and membership, was NOT traced. It may. This request is to find out by a run, not to assume either way.","body":{"conditions":"1. A real-scope witness, in the scope tests: in room A, activate a declared definition named `change` whose acts produce what `reserve` reads, establish it, and have it tell `reserve` to room B's destination with a one-file manifest. Record exactly where it stops, or that the file is published. Do the same from an application inside room B itself. 2. If either publishes or opens a publication in B: treat as critical, stop other work, and fix: at the least the destination accepts `reserve` only from a lane its own directory created (the directory already holds its lanes), and a declared definition cannot take a name that a platform handler's `from.under` names (`change`, `issue`, and any other found by grep of `under:` in packages/platform/src). 3. If both are refused: say by which guard or judgment, keep the witness as a regression test, and still close the name gap of point 2 so the refusal does not rest on a later judgment alone. 4. Report to the planner before review if the answer to 1 is that it publishes. Priority: after the lending library composition is with the checker, before hygiene work; ahead of everything if 1 shows a publication.","target":"inherit","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}